rsx draw engine: a swizzled zeta read through a swizzled texture is converted on the GPU, and stored swizzled.

Resistance 3: the light of the lamp over the radio table in Home came and went with the view (user report). Close to
the table it was gone. Close to a shadowed lamp the game renders that lamp's shadow map into a swizzled power-of-two
Z16 surface (1024x1024 or 512x512, zeta pitch register 64) and samples it as a swizzled DEPTH16 texture (format
0xD2); farther away it uses a linear one (928x928, 448x448, format 0xF2). eng_zdepth_as_color took only linear
textures of the zeta's pitch and eng_zdepth_to_guest only a pitch of at least a row, so the light shader got
whatever else was at the address: guest memory, or the bloom target that shares 0x01DA8800. Every shadowed light
then lit nothing. A per-light dump of the light buffer (scratch tooling) at one spot by the table showed it. Shadowed
lights read swizzled maps and added nothing. A step away, with linear maps, one of them lit 104k pixels.

The depth target holds the image whatever the guest layout, so a zeta rendered through a swizzled surface (raster
type 2, recorded with the write) and read through a swizzled texture is the same picture as the linear pair. The
hook now takes either matching pair; the pitch is compared for the linear one only. A mismatched pair goes through
guest memory as before. The guest store writes a swizzled zeta in Morton order over its power-of-two size
(rsx_swizzle_offset), so the fallback (a backend without the hook, PS3RECOMP_ENG_DEPTH_VIA_GUEST=1) reads the same
image too.

test_rsx_draw_engine, new test_swizzled_z16_read_as_depth16: a swizzled 256x256 Z16 zeta read as swizzled DEPTH16
goes to the hook with no readback; a linear read of it does not; a refusing backend gets the depth in Morton order
(texels (1,0), (0,1), (1,1), (2,0) at 2, 4, 6 and 8 bytes). All three fail without the change. Checked in play by the
user: walking up to the table and turning, the lamp's light stays.

Upstream: not proposed yet.

---

